1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
I've been reading your personal statement, PAUL.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:05,750
First, let's talk about your work experience in South America.

3
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:09,310
What took you there? Was it to gain more fluency in Spanish?

4
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:12,425
Well, as I'm combining Spanish with Latin American studies,

5
00:00:12,425 --> 00:00:17,270
my main idea was to find out more about the way people lived there.

6
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:20,250
My spoken Spanish was already pretty good in fact.

7
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:22,910
So you weren't too worried about language barriers?

8
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:25,940
No. In fact, I ended up teaching English there

9
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:28,080
although that wasn't my original choice of work.

10
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,270
I see. How did you find out about all this?

11
00:00:31,270 --> 00:00:35,600
I found an agency that runs all kinds of voluntary projects in South America.

12
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,000
What kind of work?

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,350
Well, there were several possibilities.

14
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:41,510
You mean construction? Engineering work?

15
00:00:41,510 --> 00:00:44,940
Yes, getting involved in building projects was an option.

16
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:48,799
Then there was tourism - taking tourists for walks around the volcanoes -

17
00:00:48,799 --> 00:00:53,710
which I actually chose to do, and then there was work with local farmers.

18
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:57,480
But you didn't continue with that project. Why not?

19
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:00,850
Because I never really knew whether I'd be needed or not.

20
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:04,711
I'd thought it might be difficult physically, but I was certainly fit enough,

21
00:01:04,711 --> 00:01:09,760
no, I wanted to do something that had more of a proper structure to it, I suppose.

22
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,680
I get de-motivated otherwise.

23
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:15,000
What do you think you learned from your experience?

24
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,940
It must have been a great opportunity to examine community life.

25
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:21,820
Yes, but it was difficult at first to be accepted by the locals.

26
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:25,932
It was a very remote village and some of them were reluctant to speak to me -

27
00:01:25,932 --> 00:01:28,800
although they were always interested in my clothes

28
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,300
and how much I'd had to pay for them.

29
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:31,700
Well, that's understandable.

30
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:33,880
Yes, but things soon improved.

31
00:01:33,880 --> 00:01:35,520
What struck me was that

32
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:38,392
when people became more comfortable with me and less suspicious,

33
00:01:38,392 --> 00:01:41,400
we really connected with each other in a meaningful way.

34
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:42,910
You made good friends?

35
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:45,480
Yes, with two of the families in particular.

36
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:49,400
Good. What about management. Did you have a project manager?

37
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:52,760
Yes and he gave me lots of advice and guidance.

38
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:54,940
And was he good at managing too?

39
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:56,840
That wasn't his strong point!

40
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:02,920
I think he was often more interested in the academic side of things than filing reports.

41
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,670
He was a bit of a dreamer.

42
00:02:04,670 --> 00:02:07,450
And did you have a contract?

43
00:02:07,450 --> 00:02:10,300
I had to stay for a minimum of three months.

44
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,120
My parents were surprised when I asked to stay longer

45
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:15,820
six months in the end. I was so happy there.

46
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:19,800
And did anything on the administration side of things surprise you

47
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:21,620
What was the food and lodging like?

48
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:24,020
Simple... but there was plenty to eat

49
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:27,510
and I only paid seven dollars a day for that which was amazing really.

50
00:02:27,510 --> 00:02:30,670
And they gave me all the equipment I needed, even a laptop.

51
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:32,400
You didn't expect that then?

52
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:32,970
No.

53
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:35,520
Well, I'll look forward to hearing more.

54
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:37,650
But now let's look at these modules.

55
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:41,910
You'll need to start thinking about which ones you will definitely want to study.

56
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:45,900
The first one here is Gender Studies in Latin America.

57
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:46,650
Mmm...

58
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:51,450
It looks at how gender analysis is reconfiguring civil society in Latin America.

59
00:02:51,450 --> 00:02:54,142
Women are increasingly occupying positions in government

60
00:02:54,142 --> 00:02:58,020
and in other elected leadership positions in Latin America

61
00:02:58,060 --> 00:02:59,910
I think you'd find it interesting.

62
00:02:59,910 --> 00:03:03,960
If it was to do with people in the villages rather than those in the public sphere, I would.

63
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:08,200
Okay. What about Second Language Acquisition?

64
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,060
Do you think I'd find that useful?

65
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:14,510
Well, you've had some practical experience in the field, I think it would be.

66
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:18,080
I hadn't thought about that. I'll put that down as a definite, then.

67
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:22,800
Okay, what about Indigenous Women's lives. That sounds appropriate.

68
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,300
I thought so too, but I looked at last year's exam questions

69
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:27,600
and that changed my mind.

70
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:30,544
Don't judge the value of the course on that.

71
00:03:30,544 --> 00:03:35,450
Maybe, talk to some other students first and we can talk about it again later.

72
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:36,250
Okay.

73
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:40,660
Yes. And lastly, will you sign up for Portuguese lessons?

74
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:44,170
My Spanish is good, so would I find that module easy?

75
00:03:44,170 --> 00:03:48,538
Not necessarily. Some people find that Spanish interferes with learning Portuguese...

76
00:03:48,538 --> 00:03:53,700
getting the accent right too. It's quite different in a lot of ways.

77
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:56,330
Well, I'd much sooner do something else, then.

78
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:59,860
Alright. Now, what we need to do is...
